index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..463c97050745ac739dcc00cf5d0fede2beaf9061
--- /dev/null
+++ b/domains/findings/pages/one-rule-is-a-check-switched-off-and-the-other-is-judged-only-when-a-value-is-answered.finding.ts
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
+
+export const oneRuleIsACheckSwitchedOffAndTheOtherIsJudgedOnlyWhenAValueIsAnswered = {
+  id: "01a07323-840d-7cb7-b8b2-7c98cbb3d3ce",
+  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
+  slug: "one-rule-is-a-check-switched-off-and-the-other-is-judged-only-when-a-value-is-answered",
+  domainSlug: "domain/akasha-check",
+  claim:
+    'Two rules go unjudged, in different ways. A computed property states `holds` and its calculation states `Held` in `Work<Page, Held>`; the two are joined by nothing but the file stem, and no check reads both. Over all 31 computed properties none disagree, and a runtime guard in `page-computing` already judges the answered value against `holds` for any page that flows through it. The manifest rule is a check already: `manifest-names-what-is-reached` states "A dependency named and not reached is the other half", implements it and carries a test. It runs on no phase — 36 of 43 code-checks run on none — and it credits every name the akasha folder holds before weighing evidence, so switched fully on it would still refuse none of the 53 unreferenced workspace declarations across 30 manifests.',
+  evidence:
+    'Measured 2026-09-05 between 13:20 and 14:20 MDT at HEAD `832fa11720`, read-only.\n\nHOLDS AND HELD, AND WHAT JOINS THEM. `pages/computed-properties/properties/holds.select-property.ts` is 26 lines; its definition is "the kind of value a calculation works out" and line 9 lists five values, `text`, `number`, `boolean`, `instant`, `date`. `pages/computed-properties/computed-property.page-type.ts:15` declares `Work<Page, Held> = (page: Page, reach: Reach) => Held | null`, and lines 38 and 42 say the calculation is what the code file beside the page exports, named `work`. The pairing is a filename convention: `X.computed-property.ts` beside `X.computed-property.code.ts`. In 30 of 31 cases the code file imports only the `Work` type and a page type, never its own page\'s value. `holds` and `Held` are two independent restatements of one fact.\n\nTHE CENSUS, AND A ZERO WITH ITS INSTRUMENT SHOWN. 31 files match `**/*.computed-property.ts` and 31 match `**/*.computed-property.code.ts`, 12 under `collections/properties/` and 19 under `alan/tracking/daily/wake-days/properties/`. All 31 paired automatically, and none disagree. 23 declare `holds: "number"` against `Held = number`; 8 declare `"text"`, 7 against `string` and one against a string union. Three seeded controls, each flipping a declared `holds` in memory, reported 1, 1 and 7 disagreements, the second exercising the alias-resolution arm, so the zero is measured rather than blind. Three pairs were hand-checked besides. What such a check could not see: `text`, `instant` and `date` are all `string` in TypeScript, and no property declares `boolean`, `instant` or `date`.\n\nWHAT DOES JUDGE IT TODAY. Not a check — `checks/` holds 43 code-checks, 45 cluster-checks and one model-check, and none reads a computed property\'s `holds`. The nearest, `calculation-imports-only-types`, says so at line 58: "Nothing here judges what a calculation exports." The gap is already named as open work: the `nimue-computed-properties` initiative carries, under the intent about refusing a calculation that reaches outside its shape, the working memory "Left here: ... that `holds` agrees with the return type." The judging is at runtime: `pages/computing/page-computing.module.code.ts:37-43` maps each of the five `holds` values to a predicate over the answered value, `date` demanding an ISO day and `number` demanding finiteness, and lines 91-105 throw where an answer fails its declared kind. That fires per page per query over values produced, so it never reads the type argument and a computed property nothing exercises goes unjudged — but it does cover the `date` and `instant` distinction a type-level check could not.\n\nTHE MANIFEST RULE IS ALREADY WRITTEN. `checks/code-checks/pages/manifest-names-what-is-reached/manifest-names-what-is-reached.code-check.ts:8` defines the check as "holding a package\'s dependencies and the packages its own code reaches to one set", line 22 states "A dependency named and not reached is the other half", and line 173 "A dependency reached by nothing is refused at its manifest\'s path." Both halves are implemented, there is a test, and it is registered at `code-check.page-type.ts:47`. Lines 11-14 set `runsOnPatch`, `runsOnWorktree`, `runsOnDeploy` and `runsOnAudit` all to false, which its page type reads at lines 118-123 as "A check running on no phase has landed and does not yet judge." 36 of the 43 code-checks are in that state.\n\nWHY TURNING IT ON WOULD NOT ANSWER THIS. `creditedIn` in its code file returns true at its first line, `if (byName.has(dep)) return true`, and `byName` is built over every manifest in the tree, so every `@akasha/*` name is credited before evidence is weighed. The page states the intent at line 88: "A dependency naming a package the akasha folder itself holds is let through." The check judges external npm packages. The one that judged workspace packages, `unused-deps.cluster-check.ts`, defined as "the check refusing a dependency a workspace declares and uses nowhere", was removed at `fb4f9be82c` of 2026-09-03, and was already dead before that.\n\nTHE COUNT, AND WHY IT MOVES. Over 291 manifests outside `node_modules`, 1497 dependency declarations and 1072 workspace declarations across 205 packages, 53 workspace declarations are reached by nothing in their own package, spread over 30 manifests. 23 of the 53 are one false-positive class: `@akasha/temper-eso-types` and `@akasha/temper-addon-library-types` carry ambient `.d.ts` files with no top-level import, so they work globally once any file includes them. The other 30 include `alan/web` naming `day`, `design-forms`, `sms-access`, `sms-core` and `status-bar-access`, each with no reference outside the manifest. The number is a reading at a moving HEAD: three runs this afternoon gave 61, 56 and 53 as a swarm landed commits. `d94d51a7e1` of 2026-09-05 11:36:44 -0600 did remove `@akasha/utils-sync` from `alan/web/package.json`, one line — but that commit is the deletion of `pages/formula`, 24 files and 1578 lines, and the manifest line went along with it rather than being noticed on its own.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT ESTABLISH. The census counts any textual occurrence of a dependency\'s name, so a name kept alive only by a stale comment reads as used and the figure under-reports. A package reached only by a sibling\'s build config outside its own folder would be missed. I did not judge whether the ambient-types convention is meant, nor whether the root manifest naming `temper-addon-data` is an install aggregation. I did not run either check.',
+} as const satisfies Finding
