index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3ee8a19d51e3eba8fdff3afac61d7e1cc7fb4c54
--- /dev/null
+++ b/domains/findings/pages/the-root-tsconfig-names-no-file-and-the-hook-guarding-it-still-says-that-run-exits-zero.finding.ts
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
+
+export const theRootTsconfigNamesNoFileAndTheHookGuardingItStillSaysThatRunExitsZero = {
+  id: "01a07323-840d-765e-b236-257f922a0d2e",
+  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
+  slug: "the-root-tsconfig-names-no-file-and-the-hook-guarding-it-still-says-that-run-exits-zero",
+  domainSlug: "domain/instrument",
+  claim:
+    '`tsconfig.json` at the repository root is two lines, `{ "files": [] }` — no `include`, no `references`, no `compilerOptions` — so it names nothing to compile. It does not report clean over that nothing: `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` exits 2 with TS18002, and the root `typecheck` script, `bunx @typescript/native-preview -b`, exits 2 with the same error. `block-typecheck.agent-hook.ts:48` states as a constraint that such a run "compiles no file and exits 0", which stopped being true at `0c8dc795c8`, where the four `references` the config carried were taken away. Of nine tsconfigs the root alone carries the empty list. `akasha typecheck` judges the files named and every file importing them, and its `--seeded` control puts a fault into each named file in memory and refuses where that file draws no diagnostic. Nothing in the tree tells a reader to run `tsc`.',
+  evidence:
+    'Measured 2026-09-05 at HEAD `832fa11720`, read-only, from the checkout root.\n\nTHE CONFIG AND WHAT IT DOES. `git show HEAD:tsconfig.json` is the whole of `{\\n  "files": []\\n}`. `bunx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` answers `tsconfig.json(2,12): error TS18002: The \'files\' list in config file \'/var/home/walton/repos/akasha/tsconfig.json\' is empty.` and exits 2. `bunx @typescript/native-preview -b`, which is verbatim what `package.json:85` binds the root `typecheck` script to, answers the same line and exits 2. So both obvious instruments fail loudly rather than passing quietly. That is the correction I owe the brief I was given, which held that the run reports clean and an agent gets a clean-looking answer; it does not, at either instrument.\n\nWHERE THE CLEAN-EXIT CLAIM ACTUALLY LIVES. It is stated inside the tree, as a `constraint` invariant on the hook that refuses `tsc`: `hook-system/agent-hooks/block-typecheck/block-typecheck.agent-hook.ts:48` reads "A `tsc` run at the repository root compiles no file and exits 0." Line 44 beside it, "The repository\'s own `tsconfig.json` names no file", still holds. Line 48 does not. `0c8dc795c8` of 2026-09-04 13:55:47 -0600 removed a `references` array naming four web projects, leaving `files: []` alone. A build-mode run over references compiles those projects; the same run over nothing is the TS18002 error. So the hook\'s warrant expired about a day before this was written, and the hook still refuses on it.\n\nTHE CENSUS. `find . -name \'tsconfig*.json\'` outside `node_modules` returns nine. Exactly one, the root, carries an empty `files`. Seven carry a non-empty `include` — four web apps and `language-design/lua-compiler/lualib` twice. The ninth, `tsconfig.base.json`, carries neither and is what the web apps extend. None of the nine now carries `references`.\n\nWHAT `AKASHA TYPECHECK` DOES INSTEAD. Its help says it judges "the files named and the files importing them", and `--seeded` is "a fault put into each file named, in memory alone, to show the run sees the file". Run over one readout file it answered `typecheck judged 7 files, the 1 file named and 6 files importing them, and none refused`, exit 0. Run over the same file with `--seeded` it answered `readout-system/readouts/pages/upkeep-sleep/upkeep-sleep.readout.code.ts — seen: 1 diagnostic with a fault seeded into it` and `nothing was written — the fault was in memory alone`, exit 0. The control asserts one thing: each file named is inside the program the run built. Where a named file draws no diagnostic with a fault in it, the command refuses with `drew no diagnostic with a fault seeded into it, so a clean answer over it means nothing`. It says of itself that it says nothing about the rest of the folder, and points at `akasha audit --check typecheck` for that. The seeded control does not cover the importer reach, only the named files.\n\nWHETHER ANYTHING SENDS A READER TO `TSC`. Nothing does. Across 723 markdown files outside `node_modules`, a grep for `tsc` as a whole word returns zero lines; the only substring hits are inside `Deutsch` and `wirtschaft`. No `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` or `README` exists in the tree, and there is no `.github/workflows`. All eight `typecheck` scripts in package manifests reach `@typescript/native-preview`, none reaches `tsc`. The word `tsc` appears on 93 lines across 16 files, and every one is either the hook refusing it, a test of that refusal, a finding page recording its blindness, a vendored `.d.ts` or `--help` text, the lockfile, or a `.gitignore` comment. So the path by which this trap would be walked into is not written down anywhere, and a hook refuses the call besides — which makes this finding smaller than it would otherwise be.\n\nA CLAIM IN THE BRIEF THAT DID NOT REPRODUCE. I was told an agent used `tsc` while landing `841c0e961b` and `9b3bbc8bb8`, reported "no readout typecheck errors", and that two files carried dangling imports at that moment. Neither commit body contains the words typecheck, compile or compiler; `841c0e961b` claims only the test suite, and `9b3bbc8bb8` says "What is left of each test file proves the reducer." Every import specifier in all sixteen touched files resolves against the git tree at both commits — fourteen relative specifiers plus eight `bun:test` externals — and the named bindings are exported too. A resolver seeded with two false specifiers reported both as dangling, so the sweep was not blind. What I could not do was run a compiler at those commits: `git worktree add` failed with `No space left on device`, so the negative rests on specifier and export resolution from git objects rather than on a compile.\n\nWHAT ELSE I DID NOT ESTABLISH. I did not run the pre-`0c8dc795c8` config, so I have not shown by measurement that the exit was ever 0; I have shown only that the references it named are gone and that the exit is 2 now. I did not judge whether the root config should name anything.',
+} as const satisfies Finding
diff --git a/domains/findings/pages/the-wide-badge-variant-union-is-read-by-one-file-and-its-eight-member-subset-by-ten.finding.ts b/domains/findings/pages/the-wide-badge-variant-union-is-read-by-one-file-and-its-eight-member-subset-by-ten.finding.ts
