+  evidence:
+    'Measured 2026-09-05 at HEAD `832fa11720`, read-only.\n\nTHE TWO RULES, QUOTED. `pages/types/page-properties/properties/property-slug.text-property.ts` carries seven invariants. Line 29: "The key a page carries is this written in camel." Line 37: "A key drops the words its declarer\'s name already says." That line was reworded from "what its declarer\'s name" to "the words its declarer\'s name" while I was writing this; I quote it as it is now. A third, line 41, reads "A qualifier only the slug\'s wider scope needed is not carried into the key", which is the dropping rule again from the other side. The page\'s definition at line 10 is "the key a page carries this property\'s value under", and its `nameFormatSlug` is `lower-kebab-case`, so the property slug is kebab and the key is not.\n\nTHE THREE PROPERTIES AND THE KEYS THAT ANSWER. `alan/harness/calendar/calendar-events/properties/calendar-event-types.text-property.ts` is slugged `calendar-event-types` at line 8 and states `propertySlug: "event-types"` at line 9; `calendar-event.page-type.ts:28` carries `eventTypes`. `collections/sync-runs/properties/run-seq.number-property.ts:9` states `propertySlug: "run-seq"`; `sync-run.page-type.ts:15` carries `runSeq`. `run-error-message.text-property.ts:9` states `propertySlug: "run-error-message"`; `sync-run.page-type.ts:24` carries `runErrorMessage`. Camelising is what answers in all three.\n\nWHAT THE OTHER ORDER YIELDS. Read the dropping rule first and `event-types` under `calendar-event` loses `event` to give `types`; `run-seq` and `run-error-message` under `sync-run` lose `run` to give `seq` and `errorMessage`. A grep for a page-type member line spelling each, across all 451 `*.page-type.ts` files, matches `types` nowhere and `errorMessage` nowhere. `seq` matches twice: `alan/tracking/session-activities/session-activity.page-type.ts:12` and `story/named-events/named-event.page-type.ts:23`. So of the three wrong keys, two name nothing and one names a real key on two page types that are not the one being asked of. A reader checking whether a key exists anywhere rather than on this page type would pass `seq`.\n\nTHE CALENDAR CASE SHOWS THE RULES HAVE NO STOPPING POINT. The dropping rule has already been applied once on that property: the page\'s slug is `calendar-event-types` and its property slug is `event-types`, with `calendar` dropped because the declarer is `calendar-event`. Applying the same rule again at the next step drops `event` and gives `types`. Nothing in the seven invariants says the rule fires once, and nothing says which of the two runs first. `sync-run` does the opposite on the same shape: `run` is what its declarer\'s name already says and `run-seq` keeps it, so the key is `runSeq`.\n\nHOW WIDESPREAD THE SHAPE IS. A script over every `*.page-type.ts` took each file\'s `slug` and each member key of its exported type, dropped the six keys inherited from `page`, and asked whether the key\'s first word is one of the slug\'s words. 451 page types carry members; 3911 keys were examined; 220 lead with a word their page type\'s slug already says. `to-do` carries eight such keys (`toDoDueDate`, `toDoPriority`, `toDoRecurrence` and five more), `monarch-account` four, `calendar-event` one (`eventTypes`). The script matches only on the first word and only against whole slug words, so it undercounts.\n\nTHE AMBIGUITY RESOLVING THE OTHER WAY, AND ITS REPAIR. `pages/queries/pages/to-dos-due.page-query.ts` named `due-date` in its `keys` list where `to-do` carries `toDoDueDate`. That is the dropping rule applied where the page type prefixes instead. It is no longer there: commit `0d65191732` of 2026-09-05 11:58:28 -0600 replaced it, and the file now names `toDoDueDate` at line 11. So this half of the brief I was given did not reproduce at HEAD, and I read it out of `git log -p` on that path instead. That commit\'s own message is the wider measurement: "Eighteen of the sixty-nine page queries name a key their page type declares